RECAP OF WHAT IS SETTLED. Fold_m sends a binary word of length m, read as the integer
N = sum a_r 2^(m-r), to the first m digits of the greedy Zeckendorf expansion of N. Write

    Phi_m(a) = ( Fold_m(a), multiset of Fold_m(a xor e_i) for i = 1..m ).

You showed that if every fibre of Phi_m has size at most 2 then the closure lemma turns any
non-discrete stable refinement into a fold-preserving involutory automorphism, and I showed
separately that the arithmetic conditions on such an involution force m into {6, 8, 9}: it
needs a Fibonacci number that is a sum of two distinct powers of two, F_k = 2^p + 2^q, with
m >= p+1 so the larger power fits the word and m <= k-3 so F_k lies beyond the retained
window, and those are compatible only for F_9 = 34 giving m = 6 and F_12 = 144 giving
m = 8, 9.

WHAT I FOUND. The fibres are not merely small. Every size-2 fibre of Phi_m, at every m I can
reach, consists of a pair differing by exactly one of the two sparse Fibonacci numbers, as
integers and not merely in their bit patterns:

    m = 6 and 7: every size-2 fibre has |a - b| = 34
    m = 8, 9, 10: every size-2 fibre has |a - b| = 144
    m = 11 through 16: no size-2 fibres at all, Phi_m is injective

So I propose the sharper statement:

    SHARP LEMMA. If Phi_m(a) = Phi_m(b) with a not equal to b, then |a - b| is 34 or 144.

This implies your two-star multiplicity theorem immediately. If a had two distinct partners b
and c, then b and c would differ by 34+34 = 68, 144-34 = 110, or 144+144 = 288, and none of
those is 34 or 144, so b and c could not share the signature with each other. Hence no fibre
of size 3 or more.

Note that the mechanism is present at m = 7 and m = 10 too, where the refinement is
nevertheless discrete: the pairs form but do not survive later rounds, because there the
condition m <= k-3 fails and F_k does disturb the retained prefix for other vertices.

